# From events to patients: how do you collapse millions of rows into one row per person?
prompt: "How do you go from one row per contact to one row per patient without losing what you need later? When do you use groupby().agg(), when transform(), and when pivot_table? Why are the first and last date per pid almost always the two most important columns in kohort?"
next: {"title":"Time is everything: washout, new users and drug coverage in pandas","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/registerdata-i-praksis-python-og-pandas/tid-er-alt-washout-ny-bruker-og.cast"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

## groupby().agg() builds the cohort
lang: en
voice: male
level: basic
use: data_table
with:
    columns:
      - pid
      - dato_inn
      - icd10
    rows:
      - [1001, '2018-03-04', E11]
      - [1001, '2019-11-22', E11]
      - [1001, '2021-06-01', I10]
      - [1002, '2017-01-09', E11]
      - [1003, '2020-08-17', E11]
      - [1003, '2020-08-30', N18]
      - [1003, '2022-02-14', E11]
      - [1003, '2023-05-03', J44]
    title: 'npr: eight rows, three patients'

    ```python py width 960 font_size 15 show above hidden true
    import pandas as pd
    pid = [1001]*3 + [1002] + [1003]*4
    codes = "E11 E11 I10 E11 E11 N18 E11 J44".split()
    dates = ["2018-03-04", "2019-11-22", "2021-06-01", "2017-01-09",
             "2020-08-17", "2020-08-30", "2022-02-14", "2023-05-03"]
    npr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": pid, "icd10": codes,
                        "dato_inn": pd.to_datetime(dates)})
    e11 = npr[npr["icd10"].str.startswith("E11")]
    kohort = npr.groupby("pid").agg(
        n_contacts=("icd10", "size"),
        n_codes=("icd10", "nunique"))
    onset = e11.groupby("pid")["dato_inn"].min()
    kohort["first_e11"] = onset.dt.date
    kohort.reset_index()
    ```

A: Now that we've seen one pid can have dozens of rows — collapsing to one row per patient is the one step in this whole course you cannot undo.
    draw title header delivery grave

A: Here is NPR in miniature: one row per contact. Patient 1001 has three — two with E11, and one with I10, high blood pressure.
    draw row_1 row_2 row_3

    pause 0.3

A: 1002 came in exactly once, in 2017 — the least dramatic patient in the cohort. 1003 has four contacts and three different codes.
    draw row_4 row_5 row_6 row_7 row_8 parallel true

B: So I just do groupby pid and first, and I have one row per patient?
    point at.ref row_5 gesture circle

A: One row, yes — but 1003 then looks like a patient with one contact and one code, and the other three rows no longer exist anywhere.
    highlight row_5 row_6 row_7 row_8 effect glow

    quiz "How many rows does kohort get when we group these eight rows by pid?"
        intro: Quick check before we code.
        right: Three — one row per unique pid, however many contacts each of them has.
        wrong: The number of rows going in doesn't matter; the number of unique pids sets the height of kohort.
        + 3
        * 8
        * 4

    clear

A: The same table in code — the dates read in as real dates, not text; on text, min() gives you the alphabetically smallest string, which happens to be right for ISO dates and never for anything else.
    draw py py_line_1 py_line_2 py_line_3 py_line_4 py_line_5 py_line_6 py_line_7 parallel true

A: First a filter: only the rows where icd10 starts with E11. That's where the onset date lives.
    draw py_line_8

A: groupby('pid') splits the table into one pile per person — nothing is computed yet.
    draw py_line_9

A: Then named aggregation: the name you want out, the source column, and the function. size counts the rows — that is, contacts.
    draw py_line_10

B: Why size and not count?
    point at.ref py_line_10 gesture underline

A: count skips missing values — if icd10 is missing on a contact, that contact drops out of your count. size counts the rows as they are.
    highlight py_line_10 effect glow delivery soft

A: nunique on the same column gives the breadth of codes — 1003 should get three.
    draw py_line_11

A: And the onset date we take from the filtered set, and attach it to kohort — pid against pid.
    draw py_line_12 py_line_13

B: Hmm — couldn't we just take the min of dato_inn across all of npr? It's the same patient.
    point at.ref py_line_12 gesture tap

A: For 1003 it happens to come out right. But had the COPD contact come first, you would have dated the diabetes onset years too early — and the whole washout window in the next lecture rests on exactly that date.
    highlight py_line_8 py_line_12 effect glow delivery grave

A: And there is kohort: three rows, one per patient — with contacts, code breadth and onset date kept.
    draw py_line_14 py_out

    pause 0.5

B: Eight rows in, three out — and none of the counting is lost.
    delivery brisk

    quiz "What is in n_codes for patient 1003?"
        intro: One last check on the table.
        right: Three — four contacts, but only three different codes: E11, N18 and J44.
        wrong: Contacts don't count here; nunique counts distinct codes, not rows.
        * 4
        + 3
        * 1

A: The named aggregation is the whole translation from events to patients: anything you don't write into it no longer exists after the collapse.
    mark mark target py_out kind box
    delivery grave

